{%- comment -%}
  Sticky buy bar. Fixed to the bottom of the viewport with safe-area inset for
  iPhone, and present at every width — the desktop buy box is sticky only for
  as long as the PDP's first section lasts, and past it there is no way to buy
  without scrolling back up.

  On the product template below 900px it is not a scroll trigger at all: it is
  on screen from the first paint and stays there, because on a phone the buy
  box is one screen out of a very long page and a control you have to scroll
  back to is a control that is missing. Above 900px, and on every other
  template, it still appears once [data-buybar-anchor] leaves the screen and
  hides again over [data-buybar-hide].

  Both add-to-cart controls are therefore on screen together on a phone, which
  the design accounts for: this one carries no title, no thumbnail and no
  price, so it reads as the page's action rather than a second copy of the buy
  box, and theme.js mirrors the buy box's quantity and variant into it so the
  two cannot disagree about what they are adding.

  No purchase logic of its own: the form carries [data-buybox-form], which is
  the same delegated handler the buy box and the product card submit through.
  Off the product page there is nothing to add, so it links to the product.

  One job: buy this. No thumbnail, no name, no price — the page above states
  all three, and a band that is on screen for the entire visit has to be
  ignorable for most of it. Anything else in it is a second thing to look at,
  every screen, whether or not the shopper is ready to act.

  That is also what lets the button be the whole bar rather than a chip at the
  end of a row: with nothing to share the width with, the CTA runs the full
  measure and is unmissable at a glance without being loud.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign p = product | default: settings.featured_product
  assign on_pdp = false
  if template.name == 'product'
    assign on_pdp = true
  endif
  assign v = p.selected_or_first_available_variant
-%}
{%- if p -%}
<div class="buybar" data-buybar role="region" aria-label="Quick purchase">
  {%- if on_pdp -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      One line of facts, directly above the button, on the product page only.

      These are not new claims. Below 900px the announcement bar carries the
      same two promises at the very top of the page, which is the wrong end of
      the visit: a shopper reads them before they have decided they want
      anything, and by the time their thumb is over Add to Cart the bar is
      several screens away. This moves them to where the hesitation actually
      happens. The announcement bar stands down on this template at this width
      so the two are never both on screen saying the same thing — see the
      setting on the announcement bar section — which also hands the first
      screen back the 32px it was spending to say it twice.

      Every string is a theme setting with no invented fallback, and the
      dispatch line ships blank. Nothing here can claim a delivery speed, a
      stock level or a follower count that the merchant has not entered, which
      is the whole difference between urgency and pressure.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- capture trust_facts -%}
      {%- if settings.dispatch_promise != blank -%}
        <li>{% render 'icon', name: 'check', size: 12 %}{{ settings.dispatch_promise }}</li>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if settings.buybar_trust_1 != blank -%}
        <li>{% render 'icon', name: 'check', size: 12 %}{{ settings.buybar_trust_1 }}</li>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if settings.buybar_trust_2 != blank -%}
        <li>{% render 'icon', name: 'check', size: 12 %}{{ settings.buybar_trust_2 }}</li>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if settings.buybar_trust_3 != blank -%}
        <li>{% render 'icon', name: 'check', size: 12 %}{{ settings.buybar_trust_3 }}</li>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endcapture -%}
    {%- assign trust_facts = trust_facts | strip -%}
    {%- if trust_facts != blank -%}
      <ul class="buybar-trust">{{ trust_facts }}</ul>
    {%- endif -%}
    <form class="buybar-form" data-buybox-form>
      <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ v.id }}">
      <input type="hidden" name="quantity" value="1">
      {%- comment -%}
        Same pending pattern as every other add-to-cart surface: both words
        ship and CSS shows one, keyed off the aria-busy the shared handler
        sets. Nothing spins and nothing animates.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <button type="submit" class="buybar-cta" data-add-to-cart {% unless v.available %}disabled{% endunless %}>
        {%- if v.available -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            A bag with a heart in it, ahead of the label. It is decorative in
            the strict sense — "Add to Cart" already says everything the bag
            says — so it is out of the accessibility tree entirely and the
            button's accessible name is unchanged.

            Nothing about the button's box moves for it: the bar's CTA is
            min-height 54px and this is 20px, so the height, radius, colour,
            width and position are all exactly what they were. The label
            re-centres around it, which is the only visible difference.

            Only on the purchase button. Off the product page this bar is a
            "View product" link, and a bag on a link that does not add
            anything to a bag would be a promise the control does not keep.
            Sold out gets no mark either, for the same reason.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {% render 'icon', name: 'bag-heart', size: 20 %}
          <span class="btn-label">Add to Cart<span class="visually-hidden"> — {{ p.title }}</span></span>
          <span class="btn-pending" aria-hidden="true">Adding&hellip;</span>
        {%- else -%}Sold out{%- endif -%}
      </button>
      {%- comment -%}
        The bar is the primary purchase control on a phone now, and the shared
        handler writes failures into whichever form was submitted. Without a
        target of its own, a refused add — a sold-out race, a dropped request —
        left the button re-enabling and no drawer opening, with nothing said to
        anyone not using a screen reader. It grows upward from a fixed element,
        so nothing in the page moves for it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p class="buybar-error" data-buybox-error role="alert" hidden></p>
    </form>
  {%- else -%}
    <a class="buybar-cta" href="{{ p.url }}">View product</a>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{ "name": "Sticky buy bar" }
{% endschema %}
